WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.940
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:02.240
I'm at the beach, and it's very pretty here,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.380
but my vacation has been completely consumed by this book,

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.140
The Parable of the Sower by Octavia Butler,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.800
which is the best book I've read in---I don't know, in years!

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.020
Like every now and again I'll read a book

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.800
and literally feel like a spell has been cast upon me

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.000
that allows me to escape the prison of myself,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.800
and for a little while I will not feel stuck inside my own consciousness,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.960
but instead I'll feel like I have magical access to worlds outside of me,

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.580
and I find that feeling to be a tremendous consolation.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.080
And then when the book ends, I'm left with this weird wobby-sobby feeling,

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.840
like I'm overwhelmed with the mysterium tremendum of having seen and having been seen.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.260
And I also feel this big sadness in my chest,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.580
maybe partly because I know I'll never get to read that book again for the first time,

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.540
but also partly because I don't know how long it'll be

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.560
until I come across a novel again that properly turns me inside out.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.960
Now of course, Hank, the books that leave me floored

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.760
aren't always gonna be the ones that leave you floored,

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.260
but I do think you would love this particular book if you haven't read it already.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.800
The Parable of the Sower is a hard book to summarize- it's a coming of age story,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.180
it's also a dystopian novel about the collapse of the United States,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.200
it's about race and class and gender and wealth,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.760
and it's also about how religions form.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.020
And even though it's more than 20 years old, Hank, it is terrifyingly contemporary.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.260
Right, but so anyway, throughout this very page-turny, roaring adventurey novel,

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.460
the narrator writes short poems about her understanding of God,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.060
which she defines as change.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.060
I usually dislike that kind of novelistic device,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.020
but Butler is such a beautiful writer that it succeeds brilliantly.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.660
Like, consider this magnificent short poem, Hank:

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.400
"In order to rise from its own ashes, a phoenix first must burn."

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.900
I want to share just two more lines with you.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.860
First, like The Great Gatsby, The Parable of the Sower begins with a fictional epigraph

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.260
which concludes, "Without positive obsession, there is nothing at all."

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.160
Hank, one of my favorite things about you is your positive obsession,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.600
AKA your nerdiness.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.600
Like, you have this ability to be unironically enthusiastic

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.640
and to find stuff that you can be strongly in favor of,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.400
and you aren't the kind of person who tears down without building up.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.460
Like when we were in high school, I felt like in order to survive

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.180
I had to create ironic distance between myself and the world.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.120
You know, like if you told me you liked a band, I would be like,

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.500
"Yeah, their old stuff was good, but now they've sold out."

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.000
Or if you told me you liked an author,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.300
I would tell you what was wrong with their work, etcetera.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.240
But Hank, you've encouraged me to let go of that, and throughout my entire adult life,

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.800
you've really been a model for positive obsession,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.240
kind of even a guiding star at times, so thank you.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.860
The second line is a simple one. As the narrator's life is falling apart, she writes,

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.960
"Kindness eases change."

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.180
Hank, I'm really sorry that Lemon is dying.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.580
I know that she's just a dog, but she's a great dog.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.860
And in the comments of your video on Friday I saw so many people whose kindness

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.620
is easing this awful change.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.020
It made me think about Lemon's life.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.120
Like, she was a racing greyhound, which often isn't the best dog life,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.040
but then when she retired, she came into your life.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.980
And just as you've made Lemon's life better, she's made your life better.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.860
We often imagine the world as a zero-sum game,

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.300
as a place where good news for someone is necessarily bad news for someone else,

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.860
but I believe that kindness can change that arithmetic,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.380
and as evidence I would point to the love that Lemon's life has brought to you and to many.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.620
The Internet can be a hard place to find kindness, maybe especially right now,

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.280
but I believe it's worth fighting for because I believe it makes the world bigger

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.780
and that it eases the hard but inevitable changes that define life.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.340
Nerdfighters, thank you for your kindness.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:08.720
Hank, I love you.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.000
I love Lemon.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:10.840
I'll see you on Friday.

